.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1200 AM PDT Fri Jul 24 2026
(Today and tonight)

Drier air aloft continues to work on a compressed marine layer
overnight into Friday morning. Stratus will push inland overnight
with uncertainty in the extent and timing but expecting less
coverage than the previous couple of nights, however patchy dense
fog is still possible in the valleys, especially the Russian River
Valley in the North Bay. Stratus should also be quicker to retreat
back to the coastline, by late morning Friday as drier air in the
lower boundary layers mixes closer to the surface. Temperatures will
remain similar as yesterday and remain so into the weekend.

&&

.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1200 AM PDT Fri Jul 24 2026
(Saturday through Thursday)

The expansive dome of high pressure centered over the Four Corners
region and the persistent troughing pattern over the EPAC has us
comfortably in the middle, with steady onshore flow at the surface
and temperatures hovering right around normal for this time of year
through the weekend into the beginning of next week. There`s no
dramatic pattern change indicated in the extended guidance. We
will see gradual warming by the middle and end of next week as
Fausto drifts northwest and mid/upper level heights rise in
response to the troughing getting shifted north. See beaches
section for impacts from Fausto along our Pacific coastline for
the weekend into next week.

