.AVIATION...
(18Z TAFS)
Issued at 1037 AM PDT Fri Jul 24 2026

Outside of HAF, VFR is anticipated to prevail this afternoon at
all terminals. Stratus continues to erode even across the San
Mateo peninsula and there`s some confidence that cigs will lift at
HAF. Satellite imagery indicates a disorganized marine layer and
this increases the degree of uncertainty with regard to the return
of stratus tonight into Saturday morning. Confidence is highest
that LVK, SJC, and APC. Local effects along the Russian River
Valley should support the development of IFR ceilings/visibility
at STS, though the duration may be shorter than the previous
night. Stratus should develop along the immediate Pacific Coast
between 00Z-03Z with HAF having the greatest potential for MVFR
and IFR ceilings. The wind field should support MVFR to IFR
ceilings at OAK, but confidence is low to medium. If the marine
layer struggles to develop, VFR may prevail through the entire TAF
period. Otherwise, onshore breezes will increase this afternoon,
with gusts of 20-25 knots, especially HAF, APC, and OAK.

Vicinity of SFO...Clouds will continue to scatter this afternoon,
with VFR and winds elevating to near 20 knots. Peak wind gusts
are advertised to approach 30 knots, with a reasonable worst case
scenario gust forecast of 32-35 knots. Confidence in the ceiling
forecast is low, and it`s possible that VFR may prevail through
the entire TAF set. This seems a little too optimistic, however,
and there`s enough indication that some stratus will push through
the San Bruno Gap and impact the terminal near or just after
sunrise Saturday (though it may be intermittent). The latest TAF
set has pushed the onset time for MVFR back by a few hours.
Depending on when coastal stratus develops, additional refinements
to timing are likely. Winds will rebuild through the day on
Saturday, but peak magnitudes are expected to be closer to 25
knots.

SFO Bridge Approach...The potential to remain VFR with just a few
clouds is higher compared to SFO. Otherwise, conditions will be
similar to SFO.

Monterey Bay Terminals...VFR currently with a delay in the return
of MVFR and IFR ceilings tonight. Ceiling onset has been pushed
back as there`s indication that coastal stratus may be slower to
re-develop. There`s low confidence in the onset time of MVFR
stratus at MRY. While MVFR stratus may arrive prior to 07Z, it is
anticipated to be intermittent. There`s around a 60% chance for
cigs to lower into the IFR category at MRY. The potential for IFR
farther east drops down to under 50% at SNS and at this time MVFR, should
it transpire, is anticipated to be the predominant flight
category. VFR should make a return prior to the end of the current
valid TAF set, but trends will be monitored. Otherwise, largely
typical onshore breezes are forecast with speeds between 8 and 12
knots.


