.BEACHES...
Issued at 830 AM PDT Fri Jul 24 2026

Hurricane Fausto in the tropical Eastern Pacific will generate a
long period southwesterly swell which is forecast to reach the
coastline of the San Francisco Bay Area and Central Coast this
weekend. As such, sneaker waves and strong rip currents are the
primary concerns. However, breaking waves can reach up to 10 feet
along some south and southwest facing beaches and break points (like
Santa Cruz, Pacifica, and parts of Stinson or Ocean Beach). Sneaker
waves can have 20+ minutes in between the larger sets (breaking
waves) with significant "flat/calm looking" times between them. This
can potentially catch beachgoers by surprise and resulting in people
being swept into the cold water; hence the name "sneaker". Rip
currents are strong enough to pull the strongest swimmers away from
shore. Swim near a lifeguard if possible. Stay back from the beach,
and away from jetties, piers, beachside rocks, and other
infrastructure near the water. Never turn your back to the ocean! A
Beach Hazards Statement is in effect from Saturday morning through
Tuesday afternoon for all beaches along the Pacific Coast.

